Community Edition → you do it manually (Repeater + trial & error) → possible, but slower.Other fancy terms you saw on the page (super simple)Dangling markup injection → Sneaky cousin of XSS. When full <script> is blocked, you can still "break" the HTML so it steals data (like CSRF tokens) by leaving tags open. Less powerful than real XSS but still dangerous.
Content Security Policy (CSP) → Website's "security rules list". Tells browser: "Only allow JavaScript from these safe places". Good defense against XSS, but sometimes attackers can bypass weak CSPs.
XSS contexts → Where your input ends up (inside <p>, inside <script>, inside attribute, etc.). You need different payloads depending on the context.

